1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:04,080
April the first — the day before her first

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:05,200
cup of chai.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,640
The sun isn't up yet.

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:10,040
Meera is holding fifty thousand rupees —

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,600
years of her savings.

6
00:00:12,849 --> 00:00:15,009
And just like that, it's in the galla.

7
00:00:15,489 --> 00:00:17,409
So — whose money is it now?

8
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:18,369
Meera's?

9
00:00:18,609 --> 00:00:19,409
Or the stall's?

10
00:00:19,862 --> 00:00:22,289
The answer is… a bit of both.

11
00:00:28,153 --> 00:00:30,153
First, the answers from last time.

12
00:00:30,713 --> 00:00:31,033
One.

13
00:00:31,513 --> 00:00:33,513
Meera pays her personal phone bill from

14
00:00:33,593 --> 00:00:34,953
her own bank account.

15
00:00:35,193 --> 00:00:37,033
Does it go in the stall's books?

16
00:00:37,793 --> 00:00:38,353
No.

17
00:00:38,593 --> 00:00:40,273
Her money, her bill.

18
00:00:40,313 --> 00:00:42,353
The stall is its own person.

19
00:00:42,811 --> 00:00:43,211
Two.

20
00:00:43,611 --> 00:00:46,091
A customer asks the price of chai, then

21
00:00:46,171 --> 00:00:47,011
walks away.

22
00:00:47,291 --> 00:00:48,451
Not a transaction.

23
00:00:48,651 --> 00:00:50,091
Nothing changed hands.

24
00:00:50,547 --> 00:00:51,187
Three.

25
00:00:51,427 --> 00:00:53,107
Which report is the photo?

26
00:00:53,347 --> 00:00:54,427
The Balance Sheet.

27
00:00:54,707 --> 00:00:57,667
And today, we start filling in that photo.

28
00:00:58,726 --> 00:01:00,646
Let's start with what the stall has.

29
00:01:01,126 --> 00:01:03,206
Right now, that's fifty thousand rupees

30
00:01:03,246 --> 00:01:03,846
in the galla.

31
00:01:04,246 --> 00:01:04,846
Cash.

32
00:01:05,446 --> 00:01:06,646
And cash is useful.

33
00:01:07,046 --> 00:01:09,286
Meera can buy milk with it, pay rent with

34
00:01:09,406 --> 00:01:10,726
it, buy a cart with it.

35
00:01:11,206 --> 00:01:13,286
Anything a business has that is useful to

36
00:01:13,366 --> 00:01:14,886
it is called an asset.

37
00:01:16,166 --> 00:01:17,646
Cash is an asset.

38
00:01:17,926 --> 00:01:20,086
Soon the cart will be an asset too, and

39
00:01:20,166 --> 00:01:21,326
so will the tea leaves.

40
00:01:22,026 --> 00:01:25,146
From now on, every asset gets its own jar.

41
00:01:25,386 --> 00:01:28,306
Each jar is what accountants call an account.

42
00:01:29,106 --> 00:01:31,586
And the cash jar — the cash account?

43
00:01:31,826 --> 00:01:32,866
That's the galla.

44
00:01:33,917 --> 00:01:36,837
But accountants always ask one more question.

45
00:01:37,117 --> 00:01:38,717
Where did it come from?

46
00:01:38,877 --> 00:01:40,797
Every asset has a source.

47
00:01:40,877 --> 00:01:42,237
So let's tag the money.

48
00:01:42,998 --> 00:01:45,478
This fifty thousand came from Meera.

49
00:01:45,558 --> 00:01:47,878
The stall holds the money, but Meera has

50
00:01:47,958 --> 00:01:49,078
a claim on it.

51
00:01:49,158 --> 00:01:51,478
If the stall ever closed, this share would

52
00:01:51,558 --> 00:01:52,758
go back to her.

53
00:01:53,438 --> 00:01:55,358
The owner's share of a business is called

54
00:01:55,518 --> 00:01:56,398
equity.

55
00:01:57,238 --> 00:01:59,518
And the money an owner puts in has its own

56
00:01:59,638 --> 00:02:01,198
name — capital.

57
00:02:01,278 --> 00:02:03,998
Meera's capital: fifty thousand rupees.

58
00:02:05,063 --> 00:02:07,783
Later that morning, someone else turns up.

59
00:02:08,023 --> 00:02:08,903
Ravi Mama.

60
00:02:09,143 --> 00:02:09,783
Umbrella.

61
00:02:10,023 --> 00:02:10,943
Moustache.

62
00:02:11,143 --> 00:02:12,503
Very proud of his niece.

63
00:02:12,983 --> 00:02:15,410
He hands Meera thirty thousand rupees —

64
00:02:15,436 --> 00:02:16,103
a loan.

65
00:02:16,343 --> 00:02:18,103
She'll pay it back once the stall is doing

66
00:02:18,183 --> 00:02:18,743
well.

67
00:02:19,499 --> 00:02:22,539
The galla now holds eighty thousand rupees.

68
00:02:22,619 --> 00:02:24,979
So what tag goes on this new money?

69
00:02:25,259 --> 00:02:26,579
Not Meera's face.

70
00:02:26,779 --> 00:02:28,939
It came from Ravi Mama, and the stall has

71
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:29,939
to give it back.

72
00:02:30,819 --> 00:02:33,059
Money a business owes to someone else is

73
00:02:33,219 --> 00:02:34,579
called a liability.

74
00:02:35,419 --> 00:02:37,899
Ravi Mama's loan is a liability.

75
00:02:38,962 --> 00:02:41,682
Now, here's where a lot of people slip.

76
00:02:41,762 --> 00:02:44,322
The galla went from fifty thousand to eighty

77
00:02:44,402 --> 00:02:45,202
thousand.

78
00:02:45,282 --> 00:02:48,162
So Meera is thirty thousand rupees richer!

79
00:02:49,291 --> 00:02:49,891
Nope.

80
00:02:50,171 --> 00:02:53,051
Yes, the cash went up by thirty thousand.

81
00:02:53,131 --> 00:02:55,051
But what the stall owes went up by thirty

82
00:02:55,131 --> 00:02:56,171
thousand too.

83
00:02:56,851 --> 00:02:57,971
Meera's own share?

84
00:02:58,051 --> 00:02:59,411
Still fifty thousand.

85
00:02:59,491 --> 00:03:00,931
Not one rupee more.

86
00:03:01,811 --> 00:03:03,971
And a liability isn't a bad thing.

87
00:03:04,051 --> 00:03:06,211
It's someone else's money, working for your

88
00:03:06,451 --> 00:03:07,091
business.

89
00:03:07,171 --> 00:03:09,731
You just have to remember it isn't yours.

90
00:03:10,778 --> 00:03:12,778
So let's look at the whole picture.

91
00:03:12,858 --> 00:03:15,178
The stall has eighty thousand rupees.

92
00:03:15,258 --> 00:03:17,178
Thirty thousand of it is claimed by Ravi

93
00:03:17,218 --> 00:03:17,898
Mama.

94
00:03:17,938 --> 00:03:20,298
Fifty thousand is claimed by Meera.

95
00:03:20,378 --> 00:03:23,258
Every rupee in the business belongs to someone.

96
00:03:24,138 --> 00:03:26,858
In one sentence: what you have equals what

97
00:03:26,938 --> 00:03:29,338
you owe, plus what's yours.

98
00:03:30,018 --> 00:03:31,378
Remember that sentence.

99
00:03:31,458 --> 00:03:33,538
Next lesson, it gets a scale.

100
00:03:34,794 --> 00:03:37,234
Let's sort some things into three boxes.

101
00:03:37,434 --> 00:03:39,714
What the stall has — assets.

102
00:03:39,914 --> 00:03:41,994
What it owes — liabilities.

103
00:03:42,314 --> 00:03:44,794
And what belongs to the owner — equity.

104
00:03:45,034 --> 00:03:46,234
Khata goes first.

105
00:03:46,394 --> 00:03:47,754
The cash in the galla.

106
00:03:49,683 --> 00:03:49,923
Asset!

107
00:03:50,083 --> 00:03:52,963
The stall has it, and it's useful.

108
00:03:53,513 --> 00:03:54,393
Meera's turn.

109
00:03:54,873 --> 00:03:56,313
Ravi Mama's loan.

110
00:03:57,882 --> 00:03:58,642
Careful.

111
00:03:58,842 --> 00:04:01,162
The cash he gave is an asset.

112
00:04:01,202 --> 00:04:03,802
But the loan is what the stall owes.

113
00:04:03,882 --> 00:04:04,842
Liability.

114
00:04:05,564 --> 00:04:06,284
Your turn.

115
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:09,244
A stack of glasses Meera buys tomorrow.

116
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:13,350
Asset.

117
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:15,990
The stall will have them, and use them every

118
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:16,550
day.

119
00:04:17,119 --> 00:04:18,079
And one more.

120
00:04:18,319 --> 00:04:21,199
April's rent — she owes it, but hasn't paid

121
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:21,919
it yet.

122
00:04:25,073 --> 00:04:25,953
Liability.

123
00:04:26,433 --> 00:04:28,753
That money is owed to the landlord.

124
00:04:30,004 --> 00:04:30,404
So.

125
00:04:30,804 --> 00:04:33,204
Assets are what the business has.

126
00:04:33,284 --> 00:04:36,004
Liabilities are what it owes to others.

127
00:04:36,084 --> 00:04:38,644
Equity is what belongs to the owner.

128
00:04:38,724 --> 00:04:40,804
And every rupee in the business belongs

129
00:04:40,884 --> 00:04:41,684
to someone.

130
00:04:42,835 --> 00:04:43,475
Your turn.

131
00:04:43,635 --> 00:04:44,635
Three quick ones.

132
00:04:45,235 --> 00:04:45,715
One.

133
00:04:45,955 --> 00:04:48,115
Gopal Dairy delivers eight thousand rupees

134
00:04:48,195 --> 00:04:50,475
of milk, and Meera will pay later.

135
00:04:50,755 --> 00:04:52,995
What does the stall now have, and what does

136
00:04:53,075 --> 00:04:54,035
it owe?

137
00:04:55,915 --> 00:04:56,555
Two.

138
00:04:56,635 --> 00:04:58,795
Is Meera's personal scooter an asset of

139
00:04:58,875 --> 00:04:59,835
the stall?

140
00:05:01,715 --> 00:05:02,355
Three.

141
00:05:02,435 --> 00:05:04,675
The stall has eighty thousand rupees, and

142
00:05:04,755 --> 00:05:06,835
owes Ravi Mama thirty thousand.

143
00:05:06,915 --> 00:05:08,355
What's Meera's share?

144
00:05:10,395 --> 00:05:12,395
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

145
00:05:13,813 --> 00:05:16,693
Next time, Meera spends thirty-six thousand

146
00:05:16,773 --> 00:05:19,413
rupees on a cart, a stove and a stack of

147
00:05:19,493 --> 00:05:20,453
glasses.

148
00:05:20,613 --> 00:05:23,093
So — did she just lose thirty-six thousand

149
00:05:23,173 --> 00:05:23,893
rupees?
